  "slug": "design-glue-eb6f",
  "summary": "Preprints pair structure-guided glue and E3 recruiter design with computational binder and engineered-kinase approaches for selective control of protein function.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is deliberate engineering of protein-protein interfaces to control target fate or activity. Two glue degrader preprints exemplify this: TrIP-MGRD targets transient PPI pockets, supported by a CDK12-DDB1 cryo-EM structure and the glue C432, while a HER2-directed antibody conjugate of a CCNK glue degrader gives HER2-selective cyclin K degradation. A degron-informed study establishes DCAF10 as a degradation E3 ligase, using an optimized Pr-GFI recruiter against FKBP12 and BRD9. Computational design appears in latent-space PDZ-binding peptide design, including off-target-aware and dual-target design, and in MobGen monobodies against the SARS-CoV-2 Spike RBD. Paired Lck deep mutational scanning yields a nilotinib-sensitized variant. The shift is toward rational, selective, interface-aware design, though the items differ in modality and maturity.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
